# 📘 Mutual Fund Abbreviations – With Explanation

This document explains the most commonly used abbreviations in Mutual Fund investing in a simple way.

---

## ✅ 1. Basic Mutual Fund Terms

### MF – Mutual Fund
A pool of money collected from many investors and invested in stocks, bonds, or other securities.

### AMC – Asset Management Company
The company that creates and manages mutual fund schemes (e.g., SBI AMC, HDFC AMC).

### AUM – Assets Under Management
The total value of money that an AMC is managing across all or a specific mutual fund.

### NAV – Net Asset Value
The price of one unit of a mutual fund.
Example: If NAV = ₹50, one unit of fund costs ₹50.

### TER – Total Expense Ratio
The percentage charged annually by the fund house for managing the fund.

### KYC – Know Your Customer
A mandatory process to verify your identity before investing.

### PAN – Permanent Account Number
A government-issued unique number used for financial transactions in India.

---

## ✅ 2. Investment & Return Related

### SIP – Systematic Investment Plan
A method of investing a fixed amount regularly (monthly/weekly) in a mutual fund.

### Lumpsum
One-time bulk investment made in a mutual fund.

### STP – Systematic Transfer Plan
A plan to transfer money from one fund to another at regular intervals.

### SWP – Systematic Withdrawal Plan
Allows investors to withdraw a fixed amount regularly from their mutual fund.

### XIRR – Extended Internal Rate of Return
Used to calculate annualized return for SIP investments with multiple dates.

### CAGR – Compound Annual Growth Rate
Shows average yearly growth of an investment over a period.

---

## ✅ 3. Fund Types / Categories

### ELSS – Equity Linked Savings Scheme
Equity mutual fund that offers tax benefits under Section 80C (3-year lock-in).

### ETF – Exchange Traded Fund
A fund that trades on stock exchanges like a share.

### FOF – Fund of Funds
A mutual fund that invests in other mutual funds.

### IDCW – Income Distribution cum Capital Withdrawal
Earlier called Dividend. Returns paid to investors from profits.

### GILT Fund
Invests only in government securities (low risk, low return).

### Large Cap Fund (LV)
Invests in the top 100 large companies by market value.

### Mid Cap Fund (MC)
Invests in medium-sized companies.

### Small Cap Fund (SC)
Invests in small and growing companies (high risk & return).

### Hybrid Fund (HNF)
Invests in both equity and debt instruments.

---

## ✅ 4. Risk & Regulation

### SEBI – Securities and Exchange Board of India
Regulates mutual funds and stock markets in India.

### CRISIL
Provides ratings for mutual fund performance and credit.

### CDSL / NSDL
Depositories that maintain your investment records in demat form.

---

## ✅ 5. Charges / Load

### Entry Load (EL)
Fee charged when entering the fund (mostly abolished in India).

### Exit Load (EXL)
Fee charged when you redeem before a specific period.

### Expense Ratio (ER)
Annual fee charged by fund house to manage investments.

